{
  "id": "soviet_tea_jazz",
  "name": "Tea-jazz (1930s-50s Soviet jazz)",
  "family": "jazz",
  "lineage": "Soviet popular jazz of the 1930s to the 1950s, typified by Leonid Utyosov's Tea-Jazz (teatralny jazz, theatrical jazz) orchestra, founded in Leningrad in 1929, which mixed dance-band arrangements with song, comedy and staged numbers and reached a mass audience through the film Jolly Fellows (1934). Alexander Tsfasman's orchestra in Moscow and Eddie Rosner's State Jazz Orchestra of Belarus played swing-based dance music in the same years, before jazz was suppressed in the late 1940s.",
  "recipe": "classic tea-jazz, improv On Frame, early branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. bright high register shallow cup lead trumpet trumpet, trombone, continuous-airflow legato saxophone, grand piano, double bass, bronze bell bronze brushes-and-sticks drum kit. blanket damped 1925-1945 commercial-major electrical era office. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
